{
  "title": "Celiac injury and absorptive surface",
  "sources": [
    "CH42S37",
    "CH42S40",
    "CH42S41"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Villus surface",
      "text": "The small intestine presents an extensive epithelial surface to the lumen. These projecting villi provide the geometry needed to understand the course packet\u2019s celiac disease explanation."
    },
    {
      "start": 11.66,
      "title": "Immune injury",
      "text": "In the supplied mechanism, gluten ingestion triggers immune injury to the villous epithelium. T-cell infiltration accompanies inflammation and mucosal destruction. Watch the projections shorten and flatten. The loss of surface connects tissue injury with impaired absorption."
    },
    {
      "start": 27.86,
      "title": "Healing distinction",
      "text": "The packet calls for permanent gluten exclusion. It separately permits lactose reintroduction after villous healing. These are different instructions: recovery does not end gluten avoidance. This schematic deformation shows a mechanism, not a measured healing time or a diagnostic biopsy."
    }
  ],
